<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hawaii</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hawaiian Islands</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hawaii is an archipelago in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> and, since 1959, the 50th state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a>. Settled by Polynesian voyagers probably between about 1000 and 1200 CE, its islands were ruled by rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a> united them as the Kingdom of Hawaii by 1810. The kingdom was overthrown in 1893, and the islands were annexed by the United States in 1898.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hawaii.jpg" alt="Hawaii" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dbenbenn · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Hawaii.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Hawaii consists of 137 volcanic islands that make up almost the entire Hawaiian archipelago, spanning 1,500 miles in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> about 2,000 miles southwest of the U.S. mainland. The eight main islands, from northwest to southeast, are Niʻihau, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kauai">Kauaʻi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molokai">Molokaʻi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanai">Lānaʻi</a>, Kahoʻolawe, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a> and Hawaiʻi, after which the state is named. The archipelago is physiographically and ethnologically part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>. The name Hawaiʻi is very similar to Proto-Polynesian *Sawaiki, reconstructed as meaning homeland, and one explanation names it for Hawaiʻiloa, a figure of oral tradition said to have discovered the islands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The date of human arrival is debated. Early archaeological studies suggested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesians">Polynesians</a> from the Marquesas or Society Islands arrived as early as the 3rd century CE. Some archaeologists think a later wave from Tahiti around 1100 CE introduced a new line of high chiefs, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapu-hawaiian-culture">kapu</a> system, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a> and the building of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heiau">heiau</a>, as Hawaiian tradition tells of Paʻao. Others say no evidence supports a later influx and treat Paʻao as myth, and more recent studies place the first settlement at around 900 to 1200 CE. The islands were home to numerous independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>.</p><p>In 1778 the British explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> was the first known non-Polynesian to arrive. European and American explorers, traders and whalers followed, and introduced diseases such as syphilis, tuberculosis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/measles">measles</a>. The indigenous population fell from an estimated 300,000 in the 1770s to 60,000 in the 1850s and 24,000 in 1920, though estimates of the pre-contact figure range from 150,000 to 1.5 million. Hawaii became a unified, internationally recognised kingdom in 1810.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">Sugar plantations</a> had a strong foothold by the 1840s and were tightly controlled by American missionary families and businessmen known as the Big Five. In 1893 resident American and European capitalists and landholders overthrew the monarchy. Hawaii was an independent republic from 1894 until 12 August 1898, when it became a U.S. territory. It was attacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> on 7 December 1941 and admitted as a state on 21 August 1959. In 1993 the U.S. government formally apologised for its role in the overthrow.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Hawaii is the 50th state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, with its capital and largest city, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honolulu">Honolulu</a>, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a>, where two thirds of residents live. Hawaiian was added to the state constitution in 1978 as an official language alongside English. Kamehameha III chose Honolulu as capital for its natural harbour, the present-day Honolulu Harbor, and the previous capitals were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahaina">Lahaina</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kailua-kona">Kailua-Kona</a> on Hawaiʻi. The aboriginal culture is Polynesian, and lūʻau and hula have spread in modified form to the wider United States.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hawaii” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the late eighteenth century the <b>Hawaiian Islands</b> were divided among rival paramount chiefs, and Kahekili II of Maui had extended his rule over Oʻahu and Molokaʻi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hawaii">Kingdom of Hawaii · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Alaska and <b>Hawaii</b> became the 49th and 50th states in 1959, and the country also holds territories including Puerto Rico and Guam.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>Japanese emigrants built shrines in <b>Hawaii</b> and Brazil.</blockquote><a href="/religion/shinto">Shinto · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Hawaiian, ʻŌlelo Hawaiʻi in the language itself, is a Polynesian language of the Austronesian family, native to the <b>Hawaiian Islands</b> and the historic language of the Hawaiian people.</blockquote><a href="/language/hawaiian">Hawaiian · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>From these islands, after a long interval, voyagers set out to settle East Polynesia from about the 11th century CE, reaching the Society Islands, the Marquesas, <b>Hawaii</b>, Rapa Nui and New Zealand.</blockquote><a href="/culture/lapita-culture">Lapita culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Elman Service called chiefdoms “redistribution societies with a permanent central agency of coordination”, but when Timothy Earle restudied <b>Hawaii</b> he found the communities largely self-sufficient: chiefs handed out prestige goods to their followers rather than food to those in need.</blockquote><a href="/government/chiefdom">Chiefdom · How It Works</a></li></ul></section>
